{% lede %}
For a science writer application at Berkeley Lab's Advanced Light Source, I wrote a highlight of a new paper and drew five diagrams to carry the explanation. The lead author, Dr. Kat Armstrong, reviewed it for accuracy.
{% /lede %}

The paper describes a simple fix for a hard problem: heating a sample in a diamond anvil cell while X-rays watch it from the side. The text can say that, but the idea only clicks when you can see where the heater, the beam and the diamonds sit.

## The diagrams

The figures build on each other. First the cell itself, then the two ways to point X-rays at it, then why a heater gets in the way, then the fix, and finally the whole setup working. Every figure uses the same colors for the same things: blue diamonds, a purple sample, orange radiation and a tan heater, so a reader who learns the code in Figure 1 can read Figure 5 at a glance.
